---
/**
 * A2 — an availability page, not a study page.
 *
 * There is deliberately no figure here and no number derived from the reference extract.
 * The page states what the study is, why its exhibits are withheld, what that costs, and
 * what a reader would need in order to run it themselves. Anything else would be quoting
 * values the repository has decided not to redistribute.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../../components/Callout.astro";
import StatusTable from "../../../components/StatusTable.astro";
import { NAV, VERIFICATION } from "../../../scripts/site";
import { href } from "../../../lib/urls";

const contents = [
  { id: "what", label: "What A2 is" },
  { id: "why", label: "Why it is withheld" },
  { id: "cost", label: "What the decision costs" },
  { id: "run", label: "What you would need to run it" },
  { id: "status", label: "Status" },
];

const description =
  "A2 compares the library's PVT correlations against reference equations of state. Its inputs are not redistributed by this repository, so no A2 figure or number is published here.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="A2 — availability"
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/studies/a2/"
  nav={NAV}
  contents={contents}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Study A2 — independent PVT check</p>
  <h1>Availability: the exhibits are withheld on rights grounds</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A2 is the only study in this project that compares the code against values it did not
    produce. It is also the only one whose inputs this repository cannot redistribute.
    <strong> No figure, table or number from A2 appears anywhere on this site</strong>, and
    none will while the rights question stands.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" label="What is on this page, and what is not">
    <p>
      This is an availability record. It describes the study, the decision that withholds it
      and how a reader could run it themselves. It carries no reference-derived value, no
      reference-derived plot, and no result restated from a report that is not in the public
      tree. <strong>A2 is not independently reproducible from this repository</strong>, and
      describing it as reproducible would be the claim this page exists to avoid making.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <section id="what" aria-labelledby="what-h">
    <h2 id="what-h">What A2 is</h2>

    <p>
      An independent check of the PVT correlation set against reference equations of state
      for three pure components, entered through their true critical constants so that no
      pseudocritical correlation contaminates the attribution. Its pre-registered experiments
      are:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Deviation factor</strong> from three correlations against pure-component
        reference states for methane, carbon dioxide and nitrogen.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A control on the reference side of that comparison</strong>: the field gas
        constant, the unit chain and the molar masses the comparison rests on, recomputed
        from primary definitions rather than from the library under test.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Viscosity</strong> from the Lee-Gonzalez-Eakin correlation against the
        reference methane viscosity, with the reference density supplied directly so that the
        comparison does not inherit the deviation-factor experiment's error, plus a positive
        control on the viscosity instrument itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A bibliographic conflict turned into a number</strong>: the two values in
        circulation for a pseudocritical-pressure constant, converted from a citation
        question into a bounded difference in the pseudocritical pressure, the deviation
        factor and the gas formation volume factor.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A declared double extrapolation</strong>: a pseudocritical correlation plus an
        acid-gas correction applied to pure carbon dioxide, with the profile that bounds what
        the correction can repair at all.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The run script is in the public tree and can be read without being run. It exits
      non-zero when the reference tables are absent, which is the correct behaviour and not a
      defect: a check whose oracle is missing has not passed, and producing a result from
      nothing would be worse than stopping.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why" aria-labelledby="why-h">
    <h2 id="why-h">Why it is withheld</h2>

    <p>
      The reference values come from the NIST Chemistry WebBook, Standard Reference Database
      69. NIST Standard Reference Data is a statutory exception to the usual rule that works
      of the United States Government are in the public domain: the Standard Reference Data
      Act, Public Law 90-396, lets the Secretary of Commerce assert copyright in it, and the
      WebBook does assert it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two NIST pages were retrieved and they disagree in tone while agreeing in effect. The
      older public-law page states that none of the data may be reproduced without prior
      permission, and carries NIST's own banner saying the page is no longer being updated.
      The current licensing statement the WebBook's metadata points at does not repeat that
      sentence, but it separates Standard Reference Data from a fair-use section it scopes to
      material <em>not</em> covered by the Act. Neither page establishes that citation alone
      permits redistributing an extract, and no carve-out for small extracts or for scientific
      use was found on either.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the project publishes metadata instead of values: the exact request URLs, the
      declared units, the isotherms and pressure grid, the row counts, the per-property
      citations, and a SHA-256 digest of each file. The tables themselves are absent from the
      tree and from its history.
    </p>

    <Callout kind="superseded" title="What the previous framing got wrong">
      <p>
        <strong>[SUPERSEDED]</strong> An earlier revision of this repository framed the
        requirement as <em>attribution</em>: cite the source and the per-species reference
        equation, and the extract may travel. A field in the reference manifest was even
        called <code>attribution_required</code>, which read as if satisfying it settled the
        matter. That was wrong. Attribution and redistribution are different permissions, the
        way access and redistribution are different permissions. Citation is still required;
        it is simply not sufficient.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="note" title="What this decision is not">
      <p>
        It is a conservative publication decision and explicitly <strong>not an adjudication
        of copyright exceptions</strong>. It is not legal advice, and it is not a finding that
        redistribution would infringe. A defensible fair-use or de-minimis argument may well
        exist for a few hundred rows of thermophysical values used as a test oracle. The point
        is that this project would have to make that argument in public, about somebody else's
        asserted copyright, with no advice, and the cost of being wrong is asymmetric. The
        claim made here is the narrow one the retrieved evidence supports: permission to
        redistribute has not been established, and an unestablished permission is not a
        permission.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section id="cost" aria-labelledby="cost-h">
    <h2 id="cost-h">What the decision costs, stated plainly</h2>

    <p>
      {VERIFICATION.testsSkipped} skipped tests and one unavailable study. In the public
      verification profile, {VERIFICATION.testsCollected} tests are collected,
      {" "}{VERIFICATION.testsPassed} pass and {VERIFICATION.testsSkipped} skip; every one of
      the skips is a reference-dependent test, and the skip count is measured by the
      verification runner rather than asserted, so a test quietly added or removed changes the
      count and fails the run.
    </p>

    <p>
      The consequence for the rest of the site is the one that matters:
      <strong> the public profile is not externally validated.</strong> It shows that the code
      agrees with its own committed snapshots, its own analytic limits and its own closed
      forms. A profile that cannot see an independent reference cannot tell you the library is
      right about the physical world, and nothing on this site describes it as validated,
      benchmarked against reality, or field-proven. That is set out in full on the
      {" "}<a href={href("/methods/")}>methods page</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Stating the price is better than a stub that pretends the oracle ran.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="run" aria-labelledby="run-h">
    <h2 id="run-h">What you would need to run it yourself</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Obtain the reference data yourself</strong>, on whatever basis is available to
        you. The repository publishes the acquisition recipe as a readable script and the
        exact request parameters in a manifest, so the retrieval is reproducible without the
        repository ever holding the values.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Place the files where the test oracle looks for them</strong>, under the
        repository's reference directory. The verification profile refuses to move restricted
        data for you: it checks the digests against the committed manifest, reports the
        reference-dependent tests as not run, and tells you to place or symlink the files
        yourself. Writing restricted data into a repository tree is a decision for the person
        who holds it, not for a script.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check the digests.</strong> Each file's required SHA-256 is in the manifest.
        If your extract does not match, you have a different extract, and the comparison is a
        different comparison.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Run the external-reference profile</strong>, which requires both the path to
        your extract and a stated access basis. The access basis is recorded in the report on
        purpose: access rights and redistribution rights are different rights, and a
        verification record that does not say which one the operator had is not a record worth
        keeping.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Run A2's own script</strong> once the extract is in place. It will produce the
        study's summary and run record locally. Neither is committed here.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Cite the WebBook and the per-species reference equation recorded in the manifest
      wherever you report those values or results derived from them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="status" aria-labelledby="status-h">
    <h2 id="status-h">Status</h2>

    <StatusTable
      caption="What has and has not run for A2 in the public profile."
      captionTitle="A2"
      rows={[
        {
          check: "A2 case study",
          scope: "Correlations against reference equations of state for three pure components",
          environment: "not run anywhere in the public profile",
          state: "BLOCKED",
          note: "its reference inputs are not in this tree; the run script exits 1 rather than producing a result from nothing",
          evidence: { label: "no committed summary in the public tree" },
        },
        {
          check: "Reference-dependent tests",
          scope: `${VERIFICATION.testsSkipped} tests comparing computed deviation factors, densities and viscosities against the reference tables`,
          environment: "not run; skipped with a reason that names the missing directory",
          state: "NOT RUN",
          note: "expected in the public profile, and the count is measured rather than asserted",
          evidence: { label: "Methods", path: "/methods/" },
        },
        {
          check: "Digest verification of the reference tables",
          scope: "SHA-256 of each extract against the committed manifest",
          environment: "not applicable in the public profile",
          state: "NOT APPLICABLE",
          note: "hashing a file the tree is supposed not to have would fail by design and would tell a reader nothing; the manifest is validated as a provenance record instead",
          evidence: { label: "Methods", path: "/methods/" },
        },
        {
          check: "Publication of A2 exhibits on this site",
          scope: "Any figure, table or number derived from the reference extract",
          environment: "not applicable",
          state: "NOT APPLICABLE",
          note: "withheld by decision, not by omission",
          evidence: { label: "This page", path: "/studies/a2/" },
        },
      ]}
      id="a2-status"
    />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
